<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">17 January 1961 · Washington, D.C.</p><h1 class="sp-title">Eisenhower's Farewell Address</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>In his farewell broadcast, President Dwight D. Eisenhower, the general who had led the Allied invasion of Europe, warned the nation about the permanent arms industry that the Cold War had created.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>In the councils of government, we must guard against the acquisition of unwarranted influence, whether sought or unsought, by the military-industrial complex.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Farewell Address, 17 January 1961.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Eisenhower%27s_farewell_address" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The phrase “military-industrial complex” entered common use and has framed the debate over defence spending ever since.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-industrial-complex">Military-industrial complex</a></div></div></section>
